      "text": "To find the value of \\( y \\) given the equation \\( x^2 + x + 4 = y - 4 \\) and the value \\( x = -7 \\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute \\( x = -7 \\) into the equation \\( x^2 + x + 4 \\).\n2. Simplify the left-hand side of the equation.\n3. Solve for \\( y \\) by isolating it on one side of the equation.\n\nLet's go through these steps in detail.\n\n### Step 1: Substitute \\( x = -7 \\) into the equation\nThe given equation is:\n\\[ x^2 + x + 4 = y - 4 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( x = -7 \\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[ (-7)^2 + (-7) + 4 = y - 4 \\]\n\n### Step 2: Simplify the left-hand side\nFirst, calculate \\( (-7)^2 \\):\n\\[ (-7)^2 = 49 \\]\n\nNext, calculate \\( -7 \\):\n\\[ -7 = -7 \\]\n\nNow, add these results together along with 4:\n\\[ 49 - 7 + 4 \\]\n\n### Step 3: Perform the arithmetic\nCalculate \\( 49 - 7 \\):\n\\[ 49 - 7 = 42 \\]\n\nThen, add 4:\n\\[ 42 + 4 = 46 \\]\n\nSo, the left-hand side simplifies to 46:\n\\[ 46 = y - 4 \\]\n\n### Step 4: Solve for \\( y \\)\nTo find \\( y \\), add 4 to both sides of the equation:\n\\[ 46 + 4 = y \\]\n\nThus,\n\\[ y = 50 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( y \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{50}\n\\]",
